{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Southwell1999-two-contiguous-stacks",
  "Source": "W. H. Southwell, \"Omnidirectional mirror design with quarter-wave dielectric stacks,\" Appl. Opt. 38, 5464-5467 (1999). doi:10.1364/AO.38.005464 - Fig. 6 caption (prescription) and p. 5467 text, Eq. (7)",
  "Notes": "Omnidirectional mirror extended by a second contiguous quarter-wave stack scaled by Eq. (7)\nSpecification: Extend the omnidirectional band by adding a stack whose centre is shifted by the shift of the upper p-band edge from normal to grazing incidence (Eq. 7).\nReported: Fig. 6: R at normal incidence (solid) and p at 85 deg (dashed), 6-20 um; the omnidirectional (overlap) band is wider than Fig. 5: normal-incidence band roughly 7.5-15 um, p 85 deg roughly 7-12.3 um (read off plot). Eq. (7) check: lambda_M/lambda_{M-1} = 1/(1 - (2/pi) asin(0.25712)) = 1.1984 for nL 1.7, nH 3.4, as used in the prescription.\nNotes: AIR (LH)^10 1.0992L 1.1984(HL)^9 1.1984H SUB, expanded: 10 x (L H), then 1.0992L, then 9 x (1.1984H 1.1984L), then 1.1984H = 40 layers, L next to air. Thicknesses in QWOT at 10 um. 1.0992 = (1 + 1.1984)/2, the average joining layer.\nLayers from Fig. 6 caption (prescription) and p. 5467 text, Eq. (7); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours min(Rs, Rp) 0.999921 over 0-89 deg at 9 um; paper wider all-angle band (Figs. 6-7, plots only).",
  "Wavelength": [
    10000
  ],
